    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d699">

                <pb facs="0743" n="485" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg485"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/1383</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-07-24">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 24, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 1.10 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2527. Following is personal note from Grey
                    to me referring to my telegram No. 2522 of to-day:<anchor n="1" xml:id="d699-f1"
                    />
                </p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>I am sending you a note which had been prepared and was ready before, the
                        notes from your Government about our prize court proceedings and the <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Neches</hi> case were sent in last week.<anchor
                            n="2" xml:id="d699-f2"/> The note I now send you was therefore written
                        without reference to these later notes; but I think it well to send it, as
                        it explains more clearly than has yet been done why we have felt ourselves
                        compelled to take the measures that were initiated last March, and the
                        grounds on which we consider that they may be justified.</p>
                    <p>The notes that you have sent in about the prize court proceedings and the <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Neches</hi> case are receiving careful consideration
                        and I hope to send you an answer on both of them next week.<anchor n="3"
                            xml:id="d699-f3"/>
                    </p>
                </quote>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d699-f1" n="1" xml:id="d699-f1-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg168">p. 168</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d699-f2" n="2" xml:id="d699-f2-content">
                <p>See telegrams Nos. 1848 and 1852 of July 14 and 15 to the Ambassador in Great
                    Britain, <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg472">p.
                    472</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d699-f3" n="3" xml:id="d699-f3-content">
                <p>This communication was telegraphed a second time by the Ambassador on July 27,
                    No. 2537, as a “covering letter” to the note of July 23, with the injunction:
                    “Please consider this as closely related to the note itself.” In this second
                    version of the document there appears, in place of the concluding words “next
                    week,” a new final sentence reading: “Negotiations are pending.” (File No.
                    763.72112/1394.)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
